  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A filibuster is a U.S. Senate tactic used to prolong debate and delay or block a vote on legislation, effectively requiring a 60-vote supermajority to invoke cloture and end debate. Originating from an 1806 rule change, it allows a minority to obstruct majority action, with the longest individual speech lasting 25 hours and 5 minutes by Sen. Cory Booker in April 2025. How a Filibuster Works and Its Rules Purpose: The primary goal is to prevent a vote on a bill, resolution, or amendment, often forcing the majority party to abandon the legislation, according to Cal Poly Magazine. Cloture Motion: To overcome a filibuster, the Senate must pass a cloture motion. Currently, this requires 60 votes (three-fifths of the Senate) to limit further debate and move to a final vote, explains the Brennan Center for Justice. Modern Usage: Unlike the traditional \"talking filibuster\" (made famous by Mr. Smith Goes to Washington), modern filibusters rarely require, according to PBS Newshour senators to hold the floor with speeches, allowing them to block legislation by merely threatening a filibuster. Exceptions: The filibuster has been removed for Supreme Court nominations and most other judicial/federal appointments, according to this YouTube video. Historical Context and Records Origin: The filibuster was an unintended consequence of removing a rule in 1806 that allowed for a simple majority to end debate, notes this YouTube video. Cloture Introduction: The Senate adopted the cloture rule in 1917 to enable a two-thirds majority to end a filibuster, according to the U.S. Senate. Longest Filibuster: The record for the longest individual speech is held by Sen. Cory Booker, who spoke for 25 hours, 5 minutes, in April 2025. Civil Rights: Throughout the mid-20th century, the filibuster was heavily used by Southern senators to block civil rights legislation, notes the Center for American Progress. Key Historical Filibuster Figures Strom Thurmond: Held the floor for 24 hours and 18 minutes against the Civil Rights Act of 1957. Cory Booker: Recorded 25 hours and 5 minutes against the policies of the Trump administration in April 2025.",
